          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Kripke–Joyal semantics
            is a higher order generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantic
            interpretation proposed initially by Beth, Grzegorczyk, and Kripke for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic predicate logic
            (IPL). It provides a notion of
            %em ‘local truth’
            or
            %em ‘validity at a stage’
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            Since it is closely related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Cohen"} Paul Cohen's
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            technique in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %em forcing
            semantics. Other terms in use for it are
            %em external
            semantics,
            %em cover
            semantics or
            %em Beth–Kripke–Joyal
            semantics.
          %p
            By giving a semantics to formulas written in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            used to express
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+mathematics"} ordinary mathematics
            in a topos, the Kripke–Joyal semantics serves as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantic
            interface between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalisation"} internal (syntactic) description
            of mathematical objects using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell%E2%80%93B%C3%A9nabou+language"} Mitchell–Bénabou language
            and the external description.
          %p
            Kripke–Joyal semantics provides rules and prescriptions for semantic interpretation for general toposes but these prescriptions may simplify for special classes of toposes e.g. the rules resulting for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+toposes"} presheaf toposes
            over posets (when restricted to first-order formulas) correspond to the original notion of model for IPL considered by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saul+Kripke"} Kripke
            et al.
          %p
            Kripke–Joyal semantics for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "n-types+cover#InModels"} here
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AGH21"} Awodey, Gambino, and Hazratpour (2021)
          %h2#formulation Formulation
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            be an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell%E2%80%93B%C3%A9nabou+language"} Mitchell–Bénabou language
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            restricting ourselves mainly to formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi (x)
            with one free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "JT77"} 1977
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Borceux94"} 1994
          %p
            Recall that such a formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi (x)
            is interpreted by a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\overset{\varphi(x)}{\to}\Omega
            and gets an extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x|\varphi(x)\}
            assigned to by the pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi φ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi true
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi φ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\{x|\varphi(x)\}&amp;\to &amp; 1&amp; \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;  &amp;\downarrow &amp;\mathsf{true} \\
                  X &amp;\underset{\varphi(x)}{\to} &amp; \Omega&amp;
                  }
          %p
            Now a global element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\overset{x_0}{\to} X
            satisfies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x)
            if it is contained in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x|\varphi(x)\}
            in the sense that the following commutes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi φ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi true
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi x
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi φ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; &amp;\{x|\varphi(x)\}&amp;\to &amp; 1 &amp;\\
                  \&amp;\nearrow&amp;\downarrow &amp;  &amp;\downarrow &amp;\mathsf{true} \\
                  1&amp;\underset{x_0}{\to}&amp; X &amp;\underset{\varphi(x)}{\to} &amp; \Omega&amp;
                  }
          %p
            Kripke–Joyal semantics results from the generalization of this satisfaction relation from global elements to
            %em generalized
            elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi α
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\overset{\alpha}{\to} X
          #forcing.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A generalized element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi α
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\overset{\alpha}{\to} X
              is said to
              %em satisfy the formula
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(x)
              or more suggestively,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %em forces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊧
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi (\alpha)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              factors through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x|\varphi(x)\}
              i.e. there exists a map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              making the following commute:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi φ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ↗
                              %mi m
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                              %mi true
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi α
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi φ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi x
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi Ω
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; &amp;\{x|\varphi(x)\}&amp;\to &amp; 1&amp; \\
                    \&amp;\overset{m}\nearrow&amp;\downarrow &amp;  &amp;\downarrow &amp;\mathsf{true} \\
                    U&amp;\overset{\alpha}{\to}&amp; X &amp;\underset{\varphi(x)}{\to} &amp; \Omega&amp;
                    }
          %p
            This is the same as to say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi im
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi α
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im\,\alpha\subseteq \{x|\varphi(x)\}
            as subobjects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x|\varphi(x)\}
            represents the set of elements satisfying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            internally, the collection of global elements has in general no such representation thereby motivating the terminology ‘external semantics’.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            One can now unwind the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊧
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi (\alpha)
            recursively over the syntactic composition of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            %em Kripke–Joyal semantics
            and permits to make contact with the original rules proposed by Kripke for intuitionistic logic in 1965.
          %p These rules are often useful for translating step by step an object defined by a formula of the Mitchell–Bénabou language into a concrete mathematical object in the topos.
          %p
            First of all, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            relation is
            %em monotone
            and
            %em local
            \:
          #Monotone_Local.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:V\to U
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊧
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi (\alpha)
              then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊧
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\models \varphi (\alpha\circ f)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:V\to U
              is
              %em epic
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊧
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\models \varphi (\alpha\circ f)
              then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊧
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi (\alpha)
          %p
            The short proof can be found in MacLane–Moerdijk (
            %a{:href => "#MM94"}> 1994
            , p.304).
          %p
            Note that the proposition gives a first hint of the importance of epimorphisms or
            %em covers
            in this semantics, testimony of its geometric underpinning.
          #Kripke-Joyal.num_prop
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              be an elementary topos and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:U\to X\,
              a generalized element of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{E}
              \. The forcing relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \models
              satisfies
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ⊧
                        %mi φ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∧
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi(\alpha)\wedge \psi(\alpha)
                  iff
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ⊧
                        %mi φ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi(\alpha)
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi U
                          %mo ⊧
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \psi(\alpha)\,
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ⊧
                        %mi φ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\models \varphi(\alpha)\vee \psi(\alpha)
                  iff there exists maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:U_1\to U
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_2:U_2\to U
                  with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 2
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
