          %p
            For a proof see MacLane–Moerdijk (
            %a{:href => "#MM94"}> 1994
            , pp.305f).
          %h2#lawvere_on_kripkejoyal_semantics Lawvere on Kripke–Joyal semantics
          %blockquote
            %p To workers in algebraic geometry and analysis, it may appear somewhat excessive to detour through an elaborate Mitchell–Bénabou language which in turn requires a Kripke–Joyal semantics in order to get back at the mathematical content of a specific topos. (That sometimes-recommended procedure is strictly analogous to defining a group to be the quotient of the free group generated by itself, which analogously is sometimes useful). The key clause in that semantics was presupposed in the title ‘Quantifiers and Sheaves’, but the linear case was a theorem in Godement 1958 and indeed just expresses in terms of 20th century concepts the content of Volterra’s local existence theorem. Briefly,
            %p a) the rule of inference for existential quantification is just a symbolic expression of the universal property enjoyed by the geometric image of any map (not only in the category of sets where the axiom of choice holds, but) in any topos,
            %p b) a figure lying in such an image comes in fact only locally from figures in the domain of the map.
          %p
            Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Law00"}> 2000
            , pp.717f).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell%E2%80%93B%C3%A9nabou+language"} Mitchell–Bénabou language
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack+semantics"} stack semantics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
          %h2#references References
          %p For the origins in the semantics of intuitionistic logic consult
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                E. W. Beth,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Semantic construction of intuitionistic logic
                %strong 19
                no.13 (1956) pp.357–388.
            %li
              %p
                A. Grzegorczyk,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A philosophically plausible formal interpretation of intuitionistic logic
                %strong 26
                (1964) pp.596–601.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saul+Kripke"}> S. A. Kripke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Semantical analysis of intuitionistic logic I
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal Systems and Recursive Functions
            %li
              %p
                H. C. M. de Swart,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An intuitionistically plausible interpretation of intuitionistic logic
                %strong 42
                no. 4 (1977) pp.564–578.
            %li
              %p
                W. Veldman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An intuitionistic completeness theorem for intuitionistic predicate calculus
                %strong 41
                no. 1 (1976) pp.159–166.
          %p A categorical-constructive take on these completeness results is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"}> Erik Palmgren
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Constructive Sheaf Semantics
              %strong 43
              (1997) pp.321-327.
          %p A (non-categorical) textbook presentation of the original Kripke semantics can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Lane+Bell"}> J. Bell
              , M. Machover,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Course in Mathematical Logic
          %p A more recent overview is in
          %ul
            %li
              D. van Dalen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Intuitionistic Logic
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Blackwell Guide to Philosophical Logic
          %p
            The topos-theoretic generalization is usually attributed to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
            who observed in the early 70s that this topos semantics subsumes various notions of forcing but his work was apparently not published. An early reference is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Osius"}> G. Osius
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A note on Kripke–Joyal semantics for the internal language of topoi
              %strong 445
              (1975) pp.349–354.
          %p
            The first section of the following sums up a 1973 talk and gives an interesting informal overview of the basics involved emphazising
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} sites
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Universal Projective Geometry via Topos Theory
              %strong 9
              (1976) pp.1-24.
          %p The following texts stress the connection to Cohen and Kripke’s work
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Variable sets etendu and variable structure in topoi
            %li#Lawvere76
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Variable quantities and variable structures in topoi
          %p Most textbooks on topos theory have a section on Kripke–Joyal semantics. Particularly thorough are
          %ul
            %li#Borceux94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Handbook of Categorical Algebra vol.3
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
          %p More concise are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos Theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"}> Colin McLarty
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes
          %p Freely available online are
          %ul
            %li#Goldblatt84
              %p
                R. Goldblatt,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topoi – The Categorical Analysis of Logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bia/1403013939"} project euclid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jaap+van+Oosten"} Jaap van Oosten
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.staff.science.uu.nl/~ooste110/syllabi/toposmoeder.pdf"} pdf
          %p The above Lawvere quote stems from
          %ul
            %li#Law00
              F. W. Lawvere,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Comments on the development of topos theory
              = succeed "," do
                %em Development of Mathematics 1950–2000
              %strong 24
              (2014) pp.1–22. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/24/tr24abs.html"}> TAC
              )
          %p For a philosophical assessment and comparison to ordinary Tarski semantics see
          %ul
            %li
              Jean Petitot,
              = succeed "," do
                %em La neige est blanche ssi… Prédication et perception
              %strong 140
              (1997) pp.35–50. (
              %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/article/MSH_1997__140__35_0.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantales"} quantales
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              R. Goldblatt,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Kripke–Joyal Semantics for Noncommutative Logic in Quantales
              = succeed "," do
                %em Advances in Model Logic vol. 6
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mcs.vuw.ac.nz/~rob/papers/aiml11printed.pdf"} draft
          %p Semantics via generalized elements in categories with pullbacks is studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Elementwise semantics in categories with pull-backs
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14731"} abstract
          %p
            Kripke-Joyal semantics for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+type+theory"} Homotopy type theory
            are developed in
          %ul
            %li#AGH21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicola+Gambino"} Nicola Gambino
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sina+Hazratpour"} Sina Hazratpour
              %em Kripke-Joyal forcing for type theory and uniform fibrations
              (2021) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.14576"}> arXiv:2110.14576
              \&amp;rbrack;
